WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.062
One Halloween night in 2013, a babysitter and the kids she's watching return

00:00:04.116 --> 00:00:07.598
home from trick or treating and discover a mysterious VHS tape mixed in with

00:00:07.604 --> 00:00:08.602
their Halloween candy.

00:00:08.666 --> 00:00:12.122
The VHS tape contains three different short films with one common element.

00:00:12.186 --> 00:00:15.210
Each of them features a character that goes by Art the Clown.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:16.874
The first short is called the 9th Circle.

00:00:16.922 --> 00:00:19.678
And the first time we see Art the Clown, he's creepily, smiling at the

00:00:19.684 --> 00:00:23.274
camera in a montage where he's just one creepy clown amongst many demons,

00:00:23.322 --> 00:00:24.934
shadowy figures and whatever this is.

00:00:25.012 --> 00:00:27.106
The first time we see Art in action.

00:00:27.138 --> 00:00:30.166
He appears seemingly out of nowhere to harass a young woman waiting for her

00:00:30.188 --> 00:00:31.490
train in Realmsville Station.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:32.662
The woman ignores Art.

00:00:32.716 --> 00:00:35.874
But as we will come to learn, Art the Clown isn't someone who can be easily ignored.

00:00:35.922 --> 00:00:38.966
He literally carries a bag of tricks around with him to get

00:00:38.966 --> 00:00:39.968
people's attention.

00:00:39.968 --> 00:00:41.530
(Art honking his horn)

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.394
After politely asking him to stop, she decides to ignore him.

00:00:44.432 --> 00:00:47.466
So he saddles up next to her and hands her a flower which is crawling with bugs.

00:00:47.488 --> 00:00:50.902
Her terror makes Art laugh hysterically, but also silently.

00:00:50.966 --> 00:00:54.006
Then, as she tries to run away, Art injects her with a sedative and takes

00:00:54.048 --> 00:00:54.730
her prisoner.

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:58.126
The horn honking, the silent laughter, and the involuntary injections will

00:00:58.148 --> 00:01:00.682
remain some of Art's signature moves throughout his existence.

00:01:00.746 --> 00:01:03.534
But there's a huge difference in this short compared to everything that follows.

00:01:03.572 --> 00:01:06.634
Here, Art is literally working on behalf of the Devil.

00:01:06.682 --> 00:01:09.938
His victim wakes up alongside two other women in a dungeon filled with the

00:01:09.944 --> 00:01:11.870
aforementioned demons and hideous creatures.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.402
So is Art a demon? It would seem so here.

00:01:14.456 --> 00:01:17.762
But later on there are also indications that he might be a disturbed human

00:01:17.816 --> 00:01:19.630
gifted with some supernatural abilities.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.530
He feels pain and he does die, albeit temporarily.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:25.494
The VHS tape that appears in Little Timmy's Trick or Treating Hall is an

00:01:25.532 --> 00:01:29.174
anthology which means that three separate semi amateur filmmakers made

00:01:29.212 --> 00:01:30.930
Short films that incorporated Art the Clown.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:33.574
If this is true, then he must be something of an urban legend in this world.

00:01:33.622 --> 00:01:36.858
He's a known entity or a myth that people know about.

00:01:36.944 --> 00:01:39.347
So then who or what is Art the Clown?

00:01:39.347 --> 00:01:43.549
How can I analyze the history of a character who is left intentionally obtuse?

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:47.402
We don't know where he comes from or what he's motivated by, because he never speaks.

00:01:47.466 --> 00:01:50.654
He's just a horrendously, violent, sometimes goofy clown who hides behind

00:01:50.692 --> 00:01:51.530
his face paint.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:54.430
Even he does not seem entirely sure what he's capable of.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.698
Lucky for me, I've analyzed a horror villain just like this before.

00:01:57.784 --> 00:02:01.426
Art isn't the first brutally violent, mysterious and mute serial killer with

00:02:01.448 --> 00:02:02.290
an obscured face.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.846
That would be the original slasher Michael Myers.

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:08.306
In fact, Myers was also something of an urban legend in his town,

00:02:08.328 --> 00:02:10.306
Where local children referred to him as the Boogeyman.

00:02:10.418 --> 00:02:13.846
But as we dig further into the Art the Clown character, you'll see that the

00:02:13.868 --> 00:02:17.526
similarities between Art and Michael go deeper than general mannerisms and overall perception, 

00:02:17.548 --> 00:02:21.354
I think Art the Clown could be a Michael Myers for a new generation.

00:02:21.392 --> 00:02:24.954
To find out how, stick around to the end of this video.

00:02:25.072 --> 00:02:27.370
This video is sponsored by Factor.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:32.830
♪ Metal Music ♪

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:36.206
Welcome to Horror History, where you'll find fun little surprises in every bite.

00:02:36.228 --> 00:02:39.930
Today we are discussing Art the Clown from the Terrifier franchise.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.110
Art the Clown has evolved quite a bit over the years.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.418
Damien Leone, the creator of Art the Clown and the director of the Terrifier movies,

00:02:45.434 --> 00:02:48.126
claims that the idea for Art started with a creepy scene that he

00:02:48.148 --> 00:02:50.430
envisioned between a woman and a clown on a crowded bus.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.614
In this imagined scene, the rest of the passengers gradually exit the bus,

00:02:53.652 --> 00:02:55.634
leaving the clown alone to terrorize the woman.

00:02:55.682 --> 00:02:58.294
This idea evolved into the scene in the train station that we just talked

00:02:58.332 --> 00:02:59.410
about from The 9th Circle.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:02.786
The 9th Circle is not only the first short film featured in All Hollows Eve,

00:03:02.818 --> 00:03:06.038
it's also the first short film that Damien Leone made that featured

00:03:06.044 --> 00:03:07.382
Art the Clown Clown back in 2008.

00:03:07.436 --> 00:03:11.874
Leone described his approach I threw in everything clowns, witches, demons,

00:03:11.922 --> 00:03:14.662
monsters, everything up against the wall, hoping something would stick.

00:03:14.726 --> 00:03:17.786
Art the Clown certainly stuck, but if we want to understand what made him

00:03:17.808 --> 00:03:19.702
stand out, we should understand his inception.

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:22.858
Leone has stated in interviews that he was very aware of Pennywise when he

00:03:22.864 --> 00:03:23.782
created Art the Clown.

00:03:23.846 --> 00:03:27.198
Leone knew he couldn't top Stephen King at Pennywise's pArticular brand of

00:03:27.204 --> 00:03:29.930
creepy clown, so he created Art to be the polar opposite.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:33.242
Pennywise has hair and round features, while Art is bald with gaunt features.

00:03:33.306 --> 00:03:34.842
Pennywise is colorful.

00:03:34.906 --> 00:03:36.158
Art is black and white.

00:03:36.244 --> 00:03:38.210
Pennywise speaks, Art does not.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.346
Pennywise doesn't use weapons, but Art will use any weapons he can get his

00:03:41.368 --> 00:03:42.018
hands on.

00:03:42.104 --> 00:03:45.838
Instead of copying him, he used Pennywise as a sort of anti-inspiration.

00:03:45.934 --> 00:03:48.866
However, that doesn't mean that there aren't any similarities between

00:03:48.888 --> 00:03:49.902
Art the Clown and Pennywise.

00:03:49.966 --> 00:03:53.366
There is a hitchhiker scene in Leone's 2011 Terrifier short film that

00:03:53.388 --> 00:03:57.834
reminds me a lot of the scene from the first It movie where Pennywise flags down a driver and asks...

00:03:57.834 --> 00:03:59.151
-[Pennywise] Don't you want it?

00:03:59.151 --> 00:04:03.822
And there's even one scene where Art's eyes glow white, very reminiscent of Pennywise's Deadlights.

00:04:03.906 --> 00:04:07.194
Whether intentional or not, there certainly is some crossover between these evil clowns.

00:04:07.232 --> 00:04:09.606
As far as appearances are concerned,

00:04:09.638 --> 00:04:12.586
Leone claims that Art the Clown's look was also partially inspired by a

00:04:12.608 --> 00:04:15.358
clown that appeared in his favorite TV show, The Twilight Zone,

00:04:15.364 --> 00:04:17.722
In an episode called - Five Characters in Search of an Exit.

00:04:17.786 --> 00:04:20.906
This clown doesn't chop anyone's limbs off, but he definitely looks somewhat

00:04:20.938 --> 00:04:21.806
similar to Art.

00:04:21.908 --> 00:04:25.118
Thankfully, Leone stops short of giving Art a flower pot for a hat, but he

00:04:25.124 --> 00:04:27.626
does later try on these very stylish sunglasses.

00:04:27.738 --> 00:04:31.106
Audiences wanted more Art the Clown after the 9th Circle, so Leone put him

00:04:31.128 --> 00:04:33.534
at the center of his 2011 short film Terrifier.

00:04:33.582 --> 00:04:36.526
Leone put the short on YouTube where it got noticed by producer Jesse Badgett,

00:04:36.558 --> 00:04:39.646
who convinced Leone to put the 9th Circle and the Terrifier shorts

00:04:39.678 --> 00:04:42.594
together with one other short in a horror anthology that eventually became

00:04:42.642 --> 00:04:43.602
All Hollows Eve.

00:04:43.666 --> 00:04:46.310
Art the Clown is the glue that holds All Hollows Eve together.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.654
His brutal acts of violence, combined with Leone's expertise in special effects makeup, 

00:04:49.692 --> 00:04:52.434
helped the film stand out and garner demand for the

00:04:52.482 --> 00:04:53.266
feature length Terrifier in 2016.

00:04:53.298 --> 00:04:57.110
This was one story, and it was Art's story from top to bottom.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.590
Or I guess more appropriately, from bottom to top.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:01.630
If you've seen the movie, you know what I mean.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.686
The myth of Art the Clown continued to grow along with Leone's budget,

00:05:04.708 --> 00:05:08.286
And in 2022, Terrifier 2 really put Art the Clown and the entire franchise

00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:08.808
On the map.

00:05:08.826 --> 00:05:11.646
There's now a third Terrifier in the works, and Art has begun to pop up in

00:05:11.668 --> 00:05:12.394
popular culture.

00:05:12.442 --> 00:05:15.306
They've put his face on ugly Christmas sweaters, and he even made a cameo

00:05:15.338 --> 00:05:17.606
appearance on Pete Davidson's show, Bupkiss.

00:05:17.658 --> 00:05:20.786
Art the Clown went from being an underground YouTube slasher to sharing an

00:05:20.808 --> 00:05:24.382
IMDb page with Edie Falco and Joe Pesci in the span of a decade.

00:05:24.446 --> 00:05:27.586
I think the secret to Art's growing popularity is the simplicity of the character.

00:05:27.608 --> 00:05:30.706
He's a creepy, horrendously, violent clown dressed in black and white.

00:05:30.728 --> 00:05:32.134
We don't know much about him.

00:05:32.172 --> 00:05:35.378
He's a blank slate of a mute monster that only comes out on Halloween,

00:05:35.474 --> 00:05:38.498
putting him in the same camp as Michael Myers from the Halloween franchise.

00:05:38.594 --> 00:05:41.366
We don't know much about Michael Myers except for the fact that he's the

00:05:41.388 --> 00:05:43.418
antagonizing force in Laurie Strode's life.

00:05:43.504 --> 00:05:47.014
However, it's this open ended brand of evil that makes Michael versatile

00:05:47.062 --> 00:05:49.578
enough to tell a variety of different stories over the years.

00:05:49.664 --> 00:05:51.862
Halloween 2 is about dreams and nightmares.

00:05:51.926 --> 00:05:55.618
H20 is about avoidance and facing one's fears, and the most recent trilogy

00:05:55.654 --> 00:05:57.194
is about intergenerational trauma.

00:05:57.242 --> 00:06:00.206
If you attach too many specifics to a character like Michael Myers, it would

00:06:00.228 --> 00:06:02.190
limit the kinds of stories that could be told around him.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:05.562
Instead, his most defining characteristic is that he's brutal and evil, much like Art the Clown. 

00:06:05.626 --> 00:06:08.418
Storytellers have done this for years.

00:06:08.504 --> 00:06:11.666
Before Michael Myers, characters like Dracula, Frankenstein and the Invisible Man

00:06:11.688 --> 00:06:14.578
were used to tell all sorts of stories that reflected the

00:06:14.584 --> 00:06:15.758
culture of their time period.

00:06:15.854 --> 00:06:18.254
Outside of horror, there are characters like Mickey Mouse.

00:06:18.302 --> 00:06:20.806
The audience hardly knows anything about Mickey, except for the fact that

00:06:20.828 --> 00:06:23.970
he's a friendly mouse that somehow owns a dog and has a girlfriend.

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:26.790
Or is it wife? See, we don't even know that for sure.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.042
This allows Disney to do whatever they want with Mickey.

00:06:29.106 --> 00:06:32.210
He can star in Steamboat Willie, he can be used to tell a classic story like

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.906
The Three Musketeers, or he can be the centerpiece of something more

00:06:34.928 --> 00:06:36.374
experimental, like Fantasia.

00:06:36.422 --> 00:06:39.626
Mickey Mouse is simply a delivery system for happy stories, the same way

00:06:39.648 --> 00:06:42.630
that Art the Clown is a delivery system for hyper violence.

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:43.738
See, they're the same.

00:06:43.824 --> 00:06:45.190
Mickey equal Art the Clown.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:49.054
So with all of this in mind, let's dig in and go over everything we do know

00:06:49.092 --> 00:06:51.818
about Art the Clown to start to unravel some of his mysteries.

00:06:51.914 --> 00:06:55.598
In order to do that, we've got to take it back to Halloween night 2013 and

00:06:55.604 --> 00:06:58.510
the rest of the content on that mysterious VHS.

00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:02.235
(Impact)

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:06.758
Art the Clown appears in all three of the short films on the VHS tape, and

00:07:06.764 --> 00:07:09.686
he slowly makes his appearance known in the home of the two kids, Tia and Timmy,

00:07:09.708 --> 00:07:11.922
much to the horror of their babysitter, Sarah.

00:07:11.986 --> 00:07:14.454
We've already covered most of what Art does to the woman on the train

00:07:14.492 --> 00:07:17.126
platform in the 9th circle, but there are a few more things that I want to

00:07:17.148 --> 00:07:18.150
point out first.

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:19.990
In this short, Art looks a little different.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:23.122
We can actually see the skin of his neck between his mask and his costume.

00:07:23.186 --> 00:07:26.274
This could be a minor thing or an oversight, but it does make Art initially

00:07:26.322 --> 00:07:27.158
seem more human.

00:07:27.244 --> 00:07:28.982
Art doesn't seem human for very long.

00:07:29.036 --> 00:07:31.774
Right after he injects the woman with a sedative that knocks her out,

00:07:31.812 --> 00:07:35.006
Art appears in a more demon like form with lit up eyes, the first and last time

00:07:35.028 --> 00:07:35.566
that we see him this way.

00:07:35.588 --> 00:07:38.574
After the scene on the train platform, Art doesn't show up again

00:07:38.612 --> 00:07:41.454
until the very end of the film, when he honks his horn yet again.

00:07:41.572 --> 00:07:44.526
Everything that takes place in the 9th circle between the glowing eyes and

00:07:44.548 --> 00:07:47.070
the flashback to the horn honking seems to take place in Hell.

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:50.318
This makes sense because the 9th circle is a reference to Dante's Inferno,

00:07:50.334 --> 00:07:52.786
which is essentially a journey through the nine circles of Hell, with the

00:07:52.808 --> 00:07:55.310
9th circle being the deepest level where the Devil resides.

00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:57.966
The Devil actually makes an appearance and drinks the blood of a fetus in the short film.

00:07:57.998 --> 00:08:01.846
This begs the question, is Art the Clown a demon?

00:08:01.868 --> 00:08:05.030
If you only saw Art in this very first short film, you'd probably think so.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:08.534
There are allusions to angels and demons later on in Art's timeline,

00:08:08.572 --> 00:08:11.410
But overall, this direct line to the Devil is mostly abandoned.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:14.666
The connection to the Devil, the glowing eyes, the imperfect costume, and

00:08:14.688 --> 00:08:17.226
the fact that Art is played by a different actor in these short films can

00:08:17.248 --> 00:08:19.030
all be chalked up to artistic license.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:22.422
Remember, the character in these shorts isn't literally THE Art the Clown.

00:08:22.486 --> 00:08:25.486
They're just interpretations of the idea of Art the Clown, a character from

00:08:25.508 --> 00:08:26.266
urban legends.

00:08:26.298 --> 00:08:29.882
So the idea that Art is demonic could merely be a theory by this filmmaker.

00:08:30.026 --> 00:08:32.106
-[Timmy] See, it's just a scary movie.

00:08:32.218 --> 00:08:33.374
-[Timmy] Let's watch it.

00:08:33.492 --> 00:08:34.814
Yes, that's me.

00:08:34.932 --> 00:08:35.678
That's me.

00:08:35.764 --> 00:08:39.246
Also worth noting, Michael Myers was also interpreted to have occult origins

00:08:39.278 --> 00:08:41.070
in Halloween: The Curse of Michael Myers.

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:43.826
The premise of this chapter in the Michael Myers story is that Michael was

00:08:43.848 --> 00:08:46.994
cursed as an infant by the demonic cult of Thorn, and that's why he must

00:08:47.032 --> 00:08:47.986
kill his own family.

00:08:48.088 --> 00:08:51.462
This wasn't a popular storyline, so that aspect was not included in future timelines.

00:08:51.516 --> 00:08:55.222
Art's direct link to the Devil is also deemphasized as his story progresses.

00:08:55.276 --> 00:08:58.394
He's evil enough without the Devil, and as we're about to find out,

00:08:58.432 --> 00:09:02.540
he's got plenty of paranormal and supernatural abilities of his own.

00:09:03.022 --> 00:09:05.993
(Impact)

